From 3c02f6578dca464c3011527be21c36e60d9f84f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 13:35:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add icon shapes, numbered badges, lettered paws and guild
 marks for /tag

Tags can show a colored arrow, the stop sign or the paw today. Raid
marking wants more distinct symbols: the familiar skull / X / sword /
diamond / flame / star set, role marks, numbers for kill or crowd-control
order, a way for a charmer to mark their own pet, and a mark for each
guild, so a caller can say "kill skull, then 3, mez the moons" and
everyone sees it.

- Icon keys after '^': K skull, X red X, A gold sword, D blue diamond,
  F green flame, T purple star, M moon (mez), U lasso (pull), N lute
  (bard), H shield (tank), $ dollar, E euro. They avoid R/O/Y/G/B/W/P/S
  on purpose: an older client reads only the first key character, so a
  free letter shows the text with no shape rather than a different,
  wrong one.
- ^WP^: a wolf's head. Read as a two-character key only when exactly W
  and P are followed by a '^'; an older client shows a white arrow.
- Numbered badges ^1^ to ^12^: a white disc with block digits. "^10^" to
  "^12^" are read as two-digit keys (only when both characters are digits
  and a '^' follows, so no existing prefix changes meaning).
- Lettered paws ^P0^ to ^PZ^: the paw with a letter or digit on its pad,
  for a charmer's initial. Drawn as the paw plus a glyph shape queued at
  the same spot. An older client reads "^PK^" as a plain paw.
- Guild marks for 30 guilds (TagShapes::kGuilds): ^B<code>^ is a
  swallowtail banner in the guild's color with its code on it (^BEUR^),
  and ^I<code>^ is the guild's own icon (^IMAY^, a lightning bolt). A
  guild key is read only when the letters up to the next '^' name a
  guild, so "^Blue^" is still a blue arrow; an older client shows a blue
  arrow for a banner and only the text for an icon. /tag guilds lists
  the codes. Three guilds reuse an existing shape as their icon (the
  wolf, the euro and the dollar sign).
- Digits and letters sit on each face separately, the back copy mirrored,
  so they read correctly from either side.
- tag_shapes.cpp/.h (new): the geometry, with no DirectX dependency. Every
  shape is flat parts extruded to the same thickness as the octagon and
  paw. Parts are convex (or star-shaped about their center) and fan-
  triangulated, or carry their own triangles (the traced wolf, and a
  small ear-clipping triangulator for the concave outlines). Concave
  outlines otherwise come from overlapping parts, and detail stands
  slightly proud of the face in a dark, light, contrasting, shaded or
  eye-yellow tone. No textures.
- tag_shapes_wolf.inc (generated): the wolf is traced from Wolf Pack's
  own wolf artwork into nested layers (face, dark linework, yellow eyes,
  pupils), each filled and drawn a step prouder than the one it sits in,
  so no polygon needs holes.
- TagArrows builds the meshes at startup, colors them with the existing
  face gradients plus the accent tones, and sizes the vertex buffer to
  hold every shape at once alongside the arrow color cache. static_asserts
  keep its Shape enum in step with TagShapes::Kind.
- The shape is now picked from the tag's own color rather than after a
  nameplate color is substituted, so a nameplate color can no longer
  collide with a shape's color.
- Prettyprint, the target tooltip and /tag help name the new shapes.
- README: the new keys and examples.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Uq6P6RYLtiKKfy5UWjT7AX
